---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const postUrl = `${base}/posts/teaching-noise-where-to-go/`;
---

<Base title="Preethi Ravula — Thinking that runs.">
  <!-- Sticky in-page nav -->
  <div class="site-nav">
    <span class="nav-brand"
      >Notes in progress <span class="sep">· Issue 00 · September 2026</span
      ></span
    >
    <nav aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#about">About</a>
      <a href="#notes">Notes</a>
      <a href="#method">Method</a>
      <a href="#topics">Topics</a>
      <a href="#issue-00">Issue 00</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero" aria-label="Introduction">
    <h1>Thinking that <em>runs.</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-note">
      Working notes on AI systems, markets, scientific computing, and causal
      reasoning—from a claim, to the evidence, to an executable example.
    </p>
  </section>
  <p class="hero-sub">
    Published when there is a problem worth unpacking—without forcing every
    question into false certainty.
  </p>

  <!-- Preview row -->
  <a class="teaser-row" href={postUrl} aria-label="Preview the first note">
    <span class="teaser-label">Preview the first note ↓</span>
    <span class="teaser-main">
      <strong>Teaching Noise Where to Go</strong>
      <span>Flow matching, moving dots, and the mathematics of generation</span>
    </span>
    <span class="teaser-meta">Post 01 · Coming soon</span>
  </a>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- About -->
  <section class="bio" id="about" aria-label="About the author">
    <img src={`${base}/profile.jpg`} alt="Portrait of Preethi Ravula" />
    <div>
      <h2>Preethi Ravula</h2>
      <p class="bio-line1">
        Applied AI Researcher · Builder of enterprise agentic systems ·
        Modernizing applications and making machine intelligence rigorous and
        useful
      </p>
      <p class="bio-line2">
        Causal inference . Stochastic modeling . numerical simulation .
        high-performance computing.
      </p>
      <p class="bio-edu">
        PhD &amp; M.S - University of Maryland, College Park<br />
        B.tech, Mech. Eng. - IIT Kharagpur
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Technical notes -->
  <section id="notes" aria-label="Technical notes">
    <div class="note-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="coming-soon">Coming soon</span>
        <p class="tag-label">Generative modeling</p>
      </div>
      <article>
        <h2 class="post-title">Teaching Noise Where to Go</h2>
        <p class="post-subtitle">
          Flow matching, moving dots, and the mathematics of generation
        </p>
        <div class="post-body">
          <p>
            An image. A voice clip. A molecule. Very different outputs — but
            underneath them is the same problem: how do you get from random
            noise to something that belongs in the real world?
          </p>
          <p>
            Flow matching learns the journey, not just the destination. It
            learns a vector field: a direction of travel for every point, at
            every moment.
          </p>
          <p>
            Take image generation for example, You start with noise and need
            to turn it into something recognizable. With Flow matching, the
            question it asks is: which way should each point move?
          </p>
          <p>
            Picture a cloud of dots drifting, bending, rearranging itself
            into a spiral. The model learns the motion that gets it there not
            just the final shape.
          </p>
          <p>
            Stringing those directions together and you get a path. Follow
            that path with an ordinary differential equation, and noise
            becomes structure.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="coming-next">
          <p>Coming next: the math, the model, and the moving dots.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="discuss-link">
          <a class="back-link" href={postUrl}> Read and discuss this note → </a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Method -->
  <section class="method-lines" id="method" aria-label="How each post works">
    <p class="kicker">HOW EACH POST WORKS</p>
    <h2>Take a position. Map the terrain. Make it run.</h2>
    <p class="method-sub">
      Clear claims, visible sources, and reproducible examples.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Topics -->
  <section id="topics" aria-label="Topics">
    <h2 class="topics-title">Lines of inquiry.</h2>
    <p class="topics-intro">
      These threads grow out of work I've done, keep returning to and
      questions I'm still excited to pursue.
    </p>
    <p class="topics-eyebrow"><span class="accent">Core</span> → Field notes</p>
    <p class="topics-statement">
      <span class="accent">Different domains, one discipline:</span> making
      assumptions visible, reasoning under uncertainty, and building models
      that can be tested.
    </p>
    <hr class="topics-statement-rule" />
    <p class="kicker">CORE RESEARCH THREADS</p>
    <ol class="topics-grid">
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">01</span>
        <p class="topic-name">AI &amp; agentic systems</p>
        <p class="topic-desc">
          How AI systems are built, evaluated, deployed, and made
          reliable—from new architectures and releases to the engineering
          beyond the demo.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">02</span>
        <p class="topic-name">Markets through models</p>
        <p class="topic-desc">
          World markets treated as interacting systems: what models reveal,
          what they hide, and how to reason under uncertainty.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">03</span>
        <p class="topic-name">Scientific computing &amp; HPC</p>
        <p class="topic-desc">
          Numerical ideas, performance, parallelism, and the practical
          machinery behind compute-intensive research.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">04</span>
        <p class="topic-name">Causal AI</p>
        <p class="topic-desc">
          How to reason about interventions, causes, counterfactuals, and
          decisions—not just predictions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="kicker">FIELD NOTES</p>
    <ol class="topics-grid">
      <li>
        <span class="topic-num">05</span>
        <p class="topic-name">Books, art &amp; travel</p>
        <p class="topic-desc">
          Occasional observations from outside the technical stack—kept
          because different disciplines sharpen the questions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Publication note -->
  <section class="pub-note" id="issue-00" aria-label="Publication note">
    <div class="pub-grid">
      <div class="pub-meta">
        <div><span class="accent">Issue 00</span></div>
        <div>September 2026</div>
        <div>Publication note</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>A place to think in public</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          This notebook starts with a simple constraint: no topic is finished
          at the headline.
        </p>
        <details class="launch-note">
          <summary
            ><span class="plus">+</span>
            <span class="label-text">Read the launch note</span></summary
          >
          <div class="launch-body">
            <p>
              Most new ideas arrive already compressed: a release
              announcement, a benchmark, a market move, a striking result.
              Compression is useful for discovery, but not for understanding.
              The work begins when we ask what the claim depends on, how it
              connects to the wider field, and whether we can make the
              mechanism visible.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is the purpose of this notebook. Each post will begin with
              a point of view, then widen into a learning map, and finally
              narrow again into an executable example. The code is not
              decoration; it is where assumptions become inspectable.
            </p>
            <p>
              The center of gravity will be AI and agentic systems, world
              markets understood through models, scientific computing, and
              causal AI. Books, art, and travel will appear more
              occasionally—as another way to notice patterns, challenge
              categories, and return with better questions.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="closing-block" aria-label="Closing">
    <div class="closing-lines">
      <p>Follow the argument.</p>
      <p>Inspect the assumptions.</p>
      <p><em>Run the example.</em></p>
    </div>
    <p class="editions-note">
      New editions will appear here as the notebook grows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll(".launch-note").forEach((d) => {
      const plus = d.querySelector(".plus");
      const label = d.querySelector(".label-text");
      d.addEventListener("toggle", () => {
        if (d.open) {
          plus.textContent = "−";
          label.textContent = "Close the launch note";
        } else {
          plus.textContent = "+";
          label.textContent = "Read the launch note";
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</Base>
